A||||3||whamel||No start question||||1||
Z||000000||whamel||08-19-2000||03:12 PM||||My new '86 GT notchback (it's a GT with SE panels) that I picked up yesterday doesn't seem to want to start.  I replaced the battery, but so far I've narrowed it down to a couple potential things.  I wanted to bounce these ideas off you guys and see if maybe there's something I might have missed.<BR>1) Electrical short?  Maybe in the column.<BR>2) Starter<BR>3) Ignition module <BR>4) neutral start switch<BR>    The short I can track down - I may have other electrical problems as it is, the starter I am having checked tomorrow and I'll check the ignition module after I do a fluid change and other initial inspection stuff, but my main question is in the neutral start switch.  Is there any way to test this?  Can I jumper it and see if it works without it?  (4-spd tranny BTW)  <BR>     A quick rundown of my symtoms as best I can see so far - key in ignition, turned to on, dash lights seem to come on right (i can run them down if it is important) as far as I can tell, turn the key to start and nothing happens.  There's a whirring sound in the 'on' position when it first comes up that I'm guessing is the fuel pump or some other pump.  So that tells me that some power is getting around in the car.<BR>     The odd stuff, I found two wires that I haven't been able to track down exactly where they go just yet.  They are above the driveaxle boot, one is green and the other is black.  They spark if they hit metal (they were sitting on metal when I connected the good battery) so they appear to be live - I just can't figure out where they plug in or what they are.  I have them taped off and separated right now.<BR>Thanks,<BR>Wade ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Vader||08-19-2000||03:51 PM||||wham,<P>Does the temperature gauge "peg" when you turn the key to the start position?  If so, the ignition switch is not completely dead.  <P>I recently had a rack/sector failure in the steering column which prevented the ignition switch from being actuated far enough to close the START contacts.  (<I>Oh JOY!  The column comes out AGAIN!</I>)  Before it completely broke, I was able to operate the starter by tilting the steering wheel to its lowest position.  Might be worth a try. <P>You can also jump the switch on the clutch pedal/neutral safety to eliminate those possibilities, but be careful to make SURE you're not in gear.  <P>You can also jump the starter solenoid engage terminal ('S' terminal) directly to the battery to test the starter independent of the ignition switch, neitral safety, etc.  If the starter operates that way, it's back to the wiring and switches.<P>Later,<BR>Vader<P>------------------<BR>"<I>Make Me Bad</I>"||||reg||1
Z||000002||whamel||08-20-2000||01:27 PM||||Thanks - the temp gauge does peg when I move to start.  I got some wire to jumper the neutral start switch, but in trying to get the switch out I noticed that mine freely rocks back and forth.  I got tied up last night and didn't have time to do any more than that, but is that normal?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||whamel||08-20-2000||02:34 PM||||Well, I got it going - it turns out it was the neutral start switch.  Looks like I have some wiring issues though, when you turn off the headlights, the tail lights come on and vice versa.  Also, there's no turn signals or brake lights for some reason.  It's a strong runner though (I sneaked a quick run around the block).  I hope I can get all it's little problems fixed before the NIFE thing in September...probably going to have the most nasty Fiero there though.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
